    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carthage">Carthage</a>'s land a province in 146 BCE; Augustus united it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa-nova">Africa Nova</a> and refounded Carthage as its capital.</li><li>Its grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olive-oil">olive oil</a> and fine pottery fed and furnished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, and its cities were among the richest in the empire.</li><li>It was a heartland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a>, home to Tertullian, Cyprian and the long Donatist schism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs, about Christians tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carthage">Carthage</a> in 180, are the oldest dated Christian document written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

